			"id": "prompt-quote",
			"type": "Text",
			"name": "68s Podcast Soundbite Script",
			"config": {
				"content": "Rigid deterministic harnesses are silently killing your autonomous AI agents. We are obsessed with predictability, so we wrap probabilistic reasoning inside rigid finite state machines and hardcoded recovery paths. When an autonomous agent stumbles, an automated self-healing loop immediately steps in to patch the failure. But at enterprise scale, this logic creates a crippling bottleneck. Multi-agent workflows do not fail cleanly; they generate infinite permutations of subtle semantic drift and cascading errors. Every single recovery cycle burns expensive compute, injects massive latency, and pollutes the shared context window. Suddenly, your architecture spends eighty percent of its execution budget merely rescuing itself from its own brittle state transitions. We set out to build dynamic autonomous swarms, but we ended up architecting hyper-fragile orchestrators that collapse under their own deterministic weight. You cannot engineer scalable agency by building heavier cages."
			}
		},
